  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Vinod Kumar vs State Of Uttarakhand & Anr on 13 March, 2020",
    "facts": "By means of present criminal misc. application, moved under Section 482 Cr.P.C., applicant is seeking quashing of order dated 06.12.2014 passed by Additional Sessions Judge, Vikas Nagar, Dehradun in S.T. No.140/211 State vs. Vinod Kumar along with entire proceedings of Sessions Trial No.140/2011 State vs. Vinod Kumar under Sections 420, 272, 273 of IPC. 2. Factual matrix of the case is that respondent no.2 lodged an F.I.R. against the applicant and three others stating that on the information given by the informant the police arrested the applicant who was plying a van. On search of the vehicle, bottles of mis- branded and adulterated cold drinks were recovered. The matter was investigated and on completion of investigation, charge-sheet was filed against the applicant and three others under Sections 420, 272, 273 of IPC. On receipt of charge-sheet, learned Magistrate took cognizance in the matter and the matter was committed to the court of Sessions. By the order of Special Judicial Magistrate 1st, the sample of alleged adulterated soft drink was soft for examination before Forensic Science Laboratory. However, examination was not done by the Laboratory as the facility of examining adulterated cold-drinks was not available there. Thereafter, the said samples were sent for chemical examination before the Public Analyst U.P. Lucknow. As per report of Public Analyst, the alleged sample was found to be adulterated. Thereafter, the matter was fixed for hearing on framing of charges.",
    "arguments": "4. Learned counsel for the applicant would submit that the sample was not found noxious as such offence under Sections 272, 273 of IPC is not made out against the applicant; at the most offence under The Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 is made out. 5. Before any discussion, Section 227 of The Code of Criminal Procedure (for short, Cr.P.C.) would be apt to reproduce here, which is as under:- \"227. Discharge.-",
    "petitioner_arguments": "4. Learned counsel for the applicant would submit that the sample was not found noxious as such offence under Sections 272, 273 of IPC is not made out against the applicant; at the most offence under The Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 is made out.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "5. Before any discussion, Section 227 of The Code of Criminal Procedure (for short, Cr.P.C.) would be apt to reproduce here, which is as under:- \"227. Discharge.-"
  },
